- pastream-reopen-after-close-segfault: opening a PaStream (either constructor form), closing
  it, then opening a second PaStream in the same process segfaults inside libportaudio's ALSA
  host API - reproduces with the plain positional constructor alone (`Pa_OpenDefaultStream`
  twice, closing in between), so it is not specific to the new object-form constructor or to
  this binding's C code. Confirmed present on the unmodified `quickjs-portaudio.c` too (tested
  by stashing the doc/portaudio.md implementation changes and rebuilding). Likely an
  ALSA/PortAudio-level issue specific to this sandboxed environment (no real audio hardware,
  JACK not running, only dummy/pulse ALSA devices - see the ALSA/JACK connection errors printed
  on every Pa_Initialize()), but not yet root-caused past that.

    import * as pa from 'portaudio';
    pa.Pa_Initialize();
    new pa.PaStream(0, 2).close();
    new pa.PaStream(0, 2); // segfaults during Pa_OpenDefaultStream

- pastream-getters-no-closed-guard (quickjs-portaudio.c: js_pastream_get): unlike the methods
  (read/write/start/stop/abort/close, which now throw "PaStream is closed"), the property
  getters (active, stopped, time, cpuLoad, readAvailable, writeAvailable) call their
  PortAudio function directly on `st` with no NULL check after `.close()` has set
  `w->stream = NULL`. Several of these don't crash on a NULL PaStream* but silently return a
  wrong value instead - e.g. `.stopped` reads back `true` after close only because
  `Pa_IsStreamStopped(NULL)`'s negative PaError return happens to coerce truthy through
  `JS_NewBool()`, not because the query is meaningful. `inputLatency`/`outputLatency`/
  `sampleRate` are safe (guarded by `Pa_GetStreamInfo(st)` already returning NULL), but the
  rest are not.

    let s = new portaudio.PaStream(0, 2); s.close(); s.stopped; // true, not because it's meaningfully "stopped"

- pastream-buffer-format-not-validated (quickjs-portaudio.c: js_pastream_get_buffer,
  js_pastream_method METHOD_READ/METHOD_WRITE): `.read()`/`.write()` accept any
  ArrayBuffer/TypedArray and just extract its raw pointer+byte length - the caller's TypedArray
  kind is never checked against the stream's own negotiated `sampleFormat` (`paFloat32`/
  `paInt16`/etc, set at open time). Found while surveying every module's buffer-handling code
  for the zero-copy interop investigation (comparing against `sndfile`/`samplerate`/
  `soundtouch`, which all validate the TypedArray kind via `js_bufferview_get`/
  `js_bufferview_get_kind` in `quickjs-typedarray.h` before touching the pointer). A stream
  opened with `paFloat32` handed a `Float64Array` (or `Int16Array`, etc.) writes/reads the wrong
  number of frames and reinterprets every sample's bytes as the wrong type - no error, no crash,
  just silently wrong audio. The `frames` default (when the frame count isn't given explicitly)
  also divides `buffer.byteLength` by `Pa_GetSampleSize(w->sampleFormat)`, which is *coincidentally*
  correct only when the caller's TypedArray kind actually does match `sampleFormat`.

    import * as pa from 'portaudio';
    pa.Pa_Initialize();
    const s = new pa.PaStream({ output: { channelCount: 1, sampleFormat: pa.paFloat32 } });
    s.start();
    s.write(new Float64Array(256)); // half as many "frames" as intended, every sample garbage

- aubio-pitch-confidence-reads-zero (third_party/aubio/src/pitch/pitchyinfft.c:193, also
  affects pitchyin.c's equivalent): AubioPitch's `.confidence` getter (quickjs-aubio.c
  PROP_PITCH_CONFIDENCE, a thin passthrough to aubio_pitch_get_confidence) reads back ~0 for
  both the 'yin' and 'yinfft' methods even on a clean, high-amplitude synthetic sine tone with
  no noise - found while building xi-build.js's pitch-detection step, which originally tried to
  gate hops on confidence >= 0.8 and got zero usable readings. `aubio_pitchyinfft_get_confidence`
  computes `1 - yinfft->data[peak_pos]`; per the surrounding code in pitchyinfft.c, `peak_pos`
  should land on a genuine low-difference candidate whenever a pitch was found at all (the
  function still returns a nonzero Hz value on every hop in this repro), so a confidence of
  exactly/near 0 alongside a valid pitch reading looks inconsistent with the algorithm's own
  logic - not yet root-caused past that. Worked around in xi-build.js by dropping the confidence
  gate entirely and taking the median Hz across many hops instead (outlier-robust without it).

    import { AubioPitch } from 'aubio';
    const pitch = new AubioPitch('yinfft', 4096, 2048, 44100);
    pitch.setUnit('Hz');
    const hop = new Float32Array(2048);
    for (let i = 0; i < 2048; i++) hop[i] = 0.8 * Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * 440 * i / 44100);
    const [hz] = pitch.process(hop);
    console.log(hz, pitch.confidence); // hz ~440 (valid), confidence ~0 (unexpected)

- console-error-warn-undefined-qjsm (environment, not this project's own code): under `qjsm`
  (quickjs-ng's `-m --std` module runner) in this environment, `console.error` and
  `console.warn` are both `undefined` - only `console.log` works. Root cause traced one level:
  `qjsm`'s own bundled `console` module imports `util`, which imports `timers`, and `timers`
  fails to load (`ReferenceError: could not load module 'timers'`) on every single run,
  regardless of what script is being executed - not caused by anything in this project's own
  `quickjs-*.c` modules or `.js` scripts. Found while writing `xi-build.js`, which originally
  called `console.error()` for its stereo-downmix notice/warnings and threw `TypeError: not a
  function` the first time a real (non-mono-synthetic) test file was tried; worked around
  there by using `console.log` everywhere instead. Other existing scripts in this directory
  (e.g. `aubio-test.js`'s usage-error path) call `console.error` too and would hit the same
  throw if that code path were ever exercised.

    qjsm -e "console.log(typeof console.error, typeof console.warn)"
    // prints "undefined undefined" (and a "could not load module 'timers'" ReferenceError
    // on every invocation, unconditionally, even for this one-line script)

- labsound-disconnect-deferred-wipes-later-connect (third_party/LabSound/src/core/AudioContext.cpp:343):
  AudioContext::disconnect only enqueues a Disconnect that is requeued as FinishDisconnect and
  applied a tick later after ramping the node out (scheduleDisconnect stops its scheduler),
  while a Connect enqueued right after it is applied immediately. A disconnect() followed by
  connect() on the same node, the normal WebAudio idiom, therefore loses the new connection and
  leaves a pass-through GainNode stopped: a rack pad went silent after the first 0.5 s. Also, the
  two-argument overload disconnect(node, index) cuts the node's INPUTS, not its outputs. Worked
  around in quickjs-labsound.cpp (disconnect_now flushes queued connections and cuts the edges
  under the graph lock); the engine's deferred disconnect is still there for other callers.

    const { OfflineAudioContext } = await import('labsound');
    const ctx = new OfflineAudioContext(2, 22050, 22050);
    const osc = ctx.createOscillator(), gain = ctx.createGain();
    osc.connect(gain); gain.connect(ctx.destination); osc.start();
    // engine calls, bypassing the binding: disconnect all outputs of gain, then connect again
    // -> gain.connect(ctx.destination) is wiped by the pending disconnect and the output goes silent


- labsound-scheduler-epoch-inconsistent-start-stop (third_party/LabSound/src/core/AudioNode.cpp:236,262):
  AudioNodeScheduler::start/stop compute _startWhen/_stopWhen as _epoch + when * sampleRate, but _epoch stays
  0 until the audio thread first processes the node and then tracks the context clock. The same call therefore
  means absolute context time on a fresh node and "seconds from now" on a playing one: stop(now + 0.5) on a
  playing oscillator fired 3.5 s late after 3 s of context time. Worked around in quickjs-labsound.cpp
  (engine_when subtracts the node's epoch, read through SchedulerAccess); initialising _epoch to the context's
  current sample frame at node construction would fix it in the engine.

    const ctx = new AudioContext(); await ctx.resume(); // let currentTime reach ~3 s
    const o = ctx.createOscillator(); o.connect(ctx.destination); o.start();
    // once it is playing: engine-level stop(ctx.currentTime + 0.5) stops after ~3.5 s, not 0.5 s

- labsound-stopped-source-stuck-stopping (third_party/LabSound/src/core/AudioNode.cpp:190): some oscillators
  reach STOPPING and are never pulled again, so they never become UNSCHEDULED and their scheduler epoch stays
  frozen at the moment they stopped. Seen while playing FM voices (oscillator -> gain -> gain chain, 12-voice
  cap stopping the oldest early) on the LabSound binding; not root-caused. quickjs-labsound.cpp releases such
  nodes once their stop time has passed, but they still cost graph edges until the next sweep.

    // tonnetz/lib/synth.js Rack.chordHit([48, 52, 55, 59], ctx.currentTime, 0.6) every 200 ms for 20 s;
    // .tmp/labsound-soak.mjs shows ctx.__nodes staying bounded only because of the release sweep

- labsound-functionnode-offline-silent (third_party/LabSound/src/extended/FunctionNode.cpp:31): on an offline
  context a FunctionNode -> GainNode -> destination graph calls the function (its counter advances every quantum
  after the first) but the destination receives only zeros, while OscillatorNode -> GainNode -> destination
  renders. Not root-caused; the offline scheduler also starts sources one quantum late.

    // C++: FunctionNode writing 1.f at its first sample, start(0), offlineRender() 8 quanta: peak is 0
